course: dictionary
lesson: 15071
title: Ridicule: when laughter hurts
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ridicule is unkind laughing at someone or something to make them look silly or stupid. As a verb, to ridicule someone is to make fun of them in a cruel or mocking way. «The other children ridiculed Maya when she tripped over her own feet.» The laughing is meant to hurt her.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like «cruel» or «mocking», or people pointing and sniggering, tell you that ridicule is happening. A friend laughing with you at a joke is different. Ridicule is laughter aimed at someone to make them feel small.
explain: Ridicule can be a kind of bullying. It hurts feelings and can stop people from trying new things. «Do not let the fear of ridicule stop you from sharing your ideas.» In many stories, inventors and dreamers are ridiculed at first, and later they are proved right.
fact: Ridicule means unkind laughing at someone to make them look silly or stupid.
fact: To ridicule someone is to make fun of them in a cruel or mocking way.
fact: «Face ridicule» means to be laughed at unkindly by other people.
fact: «An object of ridicule» means someone who is laughed at by everyone in a cruel way.
fact: Ridicule can be a form of bullying, and it can stop people from trying.
exercise: «Ella was ridiculed for wearing her wellies to the party.» What does ridiculed mean? | Asked to dance with everyone | Given a gift for her smart shoes | *Laughed at unkindly to make her look silly | Hugged warmly by her friends
exercise: Which phrase means someone is laughed at by everybody in a cruel way? | A bright idea | A loud song | *An object of ridicule | A kind smile
exercise: «Inventors are often ridiculed at first, then proved right later.» What happens to inventors at first? | They are forgotten by everyone | They are asked to teach at school | *They are laughed at unkindly | They are given prizes
test: «Do not let the fear of ridicule stop you from trying.» What does ridicule mean here? | *Unkind laughing that makes someone feel silly | A loud noise in the playground | A kind word of praise | A bright, fun game
test: Which form of bullying hurts feelings and can stop people from trying? | Helping with homework | *Ridicule | Friendship | Sharing a snack
exam: Which sentence shows someone being ridiculed? | «The children shared their picnic with a smile.» | *«The children laughed and pointed at Tom's torn coat, making him cry.» | «Mum baked a cake for the party.» | «The bus was late this morning.»
